id "e4b3c268-1dd4-5950-ab2e-09770223774b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating both sides at $t=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482318} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e2076e-2a67-59cb-8006-dae1d2be288f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "applying the chain rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482320} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f930bb-5307-59dd-80a7-b9bdb0cade2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11948125-54a4-5bb6-9128-a9f6fecd3d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "replacing ${Φ}_{0}(0)$ with ${I}_{n}$ in $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481821} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6570167-3f96-5d88-b259-3613309f60d1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1163c64f-8eb6-5387-bd44-2f1d21a98032" :question/math-academy-id "q-336479" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(0)=c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ is obtained from the defining initial value problem for ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ by  \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by  \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is a fundamental matrix for the system, every solution can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c\\,\\text{for some constant vector }c.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{properties of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ The standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is defined as the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nThe second condition, ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},$ is exactly line $\\text{L4}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ is obtained from the defining initial value problem for ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ by $\\text{using the initial condition }{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L4}$ we know ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$ Replacing ${Φ}_{0}(0)$ with ${I}_{n}$ in the equation $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={I}_{n}c=c,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly line $\\text{L5}.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{replacing }{Φ}_{0}(0)\\text{ with }{I}_{n}\\text{ in }x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229145 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ff837c0-0ebb-5a9e-acb4-894261e0318c" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution: Homogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61796693-027e-5e34-ac41-7245117c63ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the matrix exponential" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481998} {:answer/id #uuid "67302da8-f87a-5742-b68d-b6432fc52247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the identity ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{XY}$ for matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482012} {:answer/id #uuid "aecba564-d67f-5fda-a785-7112b2e9e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aecba564-d67f-5fda-a785-7112b2e9e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482187} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ad0248d-955a-5ee8-87b5-a6a9266226fa" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482409} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba11d7e-2a61-553a-9f1e-d3dc6f191266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481924} {:answer/id #uuid "887d453d-74c3-5783-9f8d-da874648d69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}=I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482090} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd63aad-1f80-570f-9351-385e1f17ff12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{A{t}_{0}}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}=-I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba11d7e-2a61-553a-9f1e-d3dc6f191266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481924} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f300979-2c03-5bca-8541-1101107fdf73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482463} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16aae11e-7722-5d5d-a3ff-56d6a8a48158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating line $\\text{L3}$ at $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481838} {:answer/id #uuid "1edc9f2b-095b-537f-8b55-2d74f8563acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})$ into $x(t)={e}^{At}c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481858} {:answer/id #uuid "619ca3f7-48eb-583d-ab51-495c74c6bef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying line $\\text{L3}$ on the right by ${e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1edc9f2b-095b-537f-8b55-2d74f8563acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})$ into $x(t)={e}^{At}c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481858} :answer-field/id #uuid "b775a29d-3fe5-5512-9ef6-783e154724b1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f598b188-bddf-5e21-881f-0c355a5c9889" :question/math-academy-id "q-337119" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L6}$ by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Line $\\text{L5}$ gives\n\n$$\n({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c.\n$$\n\nSince $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}={I}_{n}$, the right-hand side simplifies to $c$, so\n\n$$\nc=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nSimplify once, then apply $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$, to obtain\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L6}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L6}$, and $\\text{L7}.$ From $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c.\n$$\n\nUsing $\\text{L6}$, we substitute $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})$ to obtain\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L7}.$ Thus, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L6}$ by $\\text{substituting }c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\text{ into }x(t)={e}^{At}c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can combine the exponentials:\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7}$ gives line $\\text{L8}.$ Thus, line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by $\\text{the fact that }{e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$"}]}
